From dfbc7c6bca50d634b53fd85ef386c7a36971106c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: paneiol <pane1029384756@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 08:58:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add mods support: DM_MODS_DIR overlay, mod_apply tool, iOS
 mods folder

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: Mods support: DM_MODS_DIR RomFS overlay, mod_apply code mod tool, iOS mods folder, Windows front-end mods row
Issue: none

A RomFS mod needs no rebuild: the rom mount layers it over the game's RomFS at run time. DM_MODS_DIR lists the folders that hold mods (colon-separated); every subfolder is one mod, whose romfs/ is its overlay root when it has one, and the mod folder itself when it holds data. A code-only mod (exefs/ only) adds nothing at run time and is not scanned. Names sort, so the order is stable, and the explicit DM_ROMFS_OVERLAY roots come first and win over the mods. The iOS app sets DM_MODS_DIR to Documents/mods/<title ID> and, when the build carries mods, <App>/mods/<title ID> after it.

The scratch paths are on the heap (4096 bytes each): the mount take-overs run on the game's own stack, which can be as small as 8 KB, and mount_ex writes rom_overlay_env() straight into the mount's overlay buffer instead of a 4 KB temporary.

dm/tools/mod_apply.py applies code mods before the rewrite: a replaced module is staged for patch_nso.py, and .ips/.pchtxt patches are applied to the rewritten module. A build ID mismatch, or a patch that touches a registered cut or a rewrite site, is refused (DM_MODS_ALLOW_CONFLICTS=1 applies it anyway and logs it). The iOS app also creates Documents/mods and keeps it out of device backups.
